{
	"version": 5,
	"id": "BUDr34na7QNHDv--V",
	"notes": "An Ethereal (Aun): a member of the ruling caste: physically frail, mentally formidable, and treated by the other castes with reverence. Rarely armed; his weapon is Inspiring Presence, and a Fire caste Honour Guard stands around him. Point math (see Xenos/Tau/Traits/T'au Traits.adq): T'au Physiology (17) + Ethereal Caste Lens (141) + The Greater Good (-15) = 143 points of traits, plus skills (156 points) = 299 points. Stats: ST 9, DX 11, IQ 13, HT 10, HP 9, Will 17, Per 12, Speed 5.25, Move 5, Dodge 8. Tier: elite human (200-350).\n\nThe Ethereal is built without Devotion to the Ethereals (he is its object) and without weapon skills: an Ethereal is not a fighter, and a GM who needs one can add Beam Weapons (Pistol) at DX. Honour Guard and starting wealth are not priced here; a GM who wants the Honour Guard as an Ally Group can add it.",
	"traits": [
		{
			"id": "T9tovrypjvt1ls2fT",
			"name": "T'au Physiology",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The T'au are a young race of about human size: blue-grey skin, reverse-jointed hooved legs, blunt features and a stocky-to-slender range depending on caste. They are no stronger than humans and no faster; the differences between them are made by caste and training, not by biology, so this package is deliberately thin (attributes, Warp-Faint and nothing else) and each caste lens carries its own attribute line. Codex: T'au Empire background.\n\nDesign: Warp-Faint 17, plus a disabled optional Short Lifespan (0). Total 17 points, no attribute change: ST 10, DX 10, IQ 10, HT 10 as human, so that framework.md's non-Fire castes (Water caste DX 10, Ethereal DX 11) come out exactly from their lenses. Left out: any hoof or leg-shape trait (no rules effect the drafter is confident of), Language and Cultural Familiarity (0-point when native; campaign-dependent).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"T'au"
			],
			"container_type": "ancestry",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "TQvPiVzv4d0dMCapA",
					"name": "Warp-Faint",
					"local_notes": "Psychic mind-reading, mind-control and emotion-control powers used on a T'au suffer -3; the T'au gets +8 to HT rolls against possession, warp-taint and daemonic influence. No T'au is ever a psyker: no template in this file carries Psyker (Talent), Warp Empowerment or any Warp Sorcery, and none should be given one.\n\nLore: The T'au have almost no presence in the warp. Psykers have trouble reading or dominating them, daemons find little to grip, and no T'au has ever been shown to be a psyker. This is consistent background across Codex: T'au Empire editions; how strong the effect is is left vague, and the fiction shows T'au dying to daemons and to psychic assault, so it is priced as a real but partial defence, not the total immunity a Pariah or a Necron has.\n\nDesign: Mind Shield 3 (B70, 4/level) = 12, plus Resistant (B81) at the Occasional hazard base of 10 with the +8 bonus (x1/2) = 5, for 17 points. Deliberately not Immunity: a Pariah's Immunity to Warp-Based Effects (data/imperium/psykers/warp.yaml) costs 10 for a total shield and the Necron Soulless trait does the same, but the T'au are only faint in the warp, not absent from it. 'Cannot be a psyker' is a rule of the setting rather than a priced disadvantage: nothing in a T'au build ever buys a psychic advantage, so there is nothing to price.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"T'au"
					],
					"container_type": "meta_trait",
					"children": [
						{
							"id": "t_iytNyYiwz26MkiH",
							"name": "Mind Shield (Warp-Faint)",
							"reference": "B70",
							"local_notes": "Mind Shield 3: -3 to mind-reading, mind-control and emotion-control powers used on this character.\n\nLore: Psykers find a T'au's mind slippery and dim: hard to read, harder to seize.\n\nDesign: Mind Shield 3 (B70, 4/level) = 12 points.",
							"tags": [
								"Advantage",
								"Mental",
								"Xenos",
								"T'au"
							],
							"points_per_level": 4,
							"can_level": true,
							"levels": 3
						},
						{
							"id": "tmAWgRMml48QfO5-y",
							"name": "Resistant (Warp Influence)",
							"reference": "B81",
							"local_notes": "+8 to HT rolls to resist possession, warp-taint mutation and daemonic influence.\n\nLore: A soul that barely registers in the warp gives a daemon little to take hold of.\n\nDesign: Resistant (B81): Occasional hazard 10, +8 to resist (x1/2) = 5 points.",
							"tags": [
								"Advantage",
								"Mental",
								"Xenos",
								"T'au"
							],
							"modifiers": [
								{
									"id": "mDogiq4sCjZebvanz",
									"name": "Occasional hazard: possession, warp-taint and daemonic influence",
									"reference": "B81",
									"cost_adj": "10"
								},
								{
									"id": "mpjbz2c8n_p2V9PQj",
									"name": "+8 to all HT rolls to resist",
									"reference": "B81",
									"cost_adj": "x0.5"
								}
							]
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tDnD184Xtl1kY23Of",
					"name": "Short Lifespan (T'au, optional)",
					"reference": "B154",
					"local_notes": "Disabled: charged nothing. A GM who wants the T'au to age faster than baseline humans can enable this (-10 for one level, B154).\n\nLore: Background describes the T'au as shorter-lived than humans, with Ethereals said to live longer, but the drafter could not pin a figure or a citation to that, and the framework asks for Short Lifespan only where the drafter is confident. So: left out of the price.\n\nDesign: Short Lifespan is -10 per level (B154). Not charged, so no T'au template carries the discount and the racial totals are not padded by an unsourced disadvantage.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"T'au"
					],
					"points_per_level": -10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1,
					"disabled": true
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T_mpAZWsBqvuJp03x",
			"name": "Ethereal Caste Lens",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Ethereals are the Empire's rulers: physically frail, mentally formidable, and treated by the other castes with something between reverence and worship. They seldom fight; their weapon is presence. Aun is the caste's name for itself (Aun'va was the Supreme Ruler). Codex: T'au Empire background.\n\nDesign: Attribute Modifiers (Ethereal) 85 + Status 5 (25) + Inspiring Presence 31 = 141 points. Status 5 because Ethereals sit at the top of a society ruled by them, as a monarch or a high priest would; a lower Status would understate how thoroughly the other castes defer.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"T'au"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tG0SE0e-gqzACZuaP",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Ethereal)",
					"local_notes": "ST -1, DX +1, IQ +3, Per -1, Will +4.\n\nDesign: Framework Aun line: ST 9, DX 11, IQ 13, HT 10, HP 9, Per 12, Will 17, Move 5. ST -1 (-10), DX +1 (20), IQ +3 (60), Per -1 (-5, so Per 12 with IQ 13), Will +4 (20, so Will 17 with IQ 13) = 85. Basic Speed (11+10)/4 = 5.25, Move 5, HP 9 = ST 9.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"T'au"
					],
					"base_points": 85,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": -1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": 3
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "per",
							"amount": -1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "will",
							"amount": 4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tuF65NwEq7yycHjl5",
					"name": "Status (Ethereal Caste)",
					"reference": "B28",
					"local_notes": "Status 5: rulers and near-sacred figures to most T'au.\n\nLore: The Ethereals govern the Empire; the other castes serve.\n\nDesign: Status (B28, 5/level), 5 levels = 25 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Social",
						"Xenos",
						"T'au"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 5
				},
				{
					"id": "TpEEMgsi1y1kRTN1y",
					"name": "Inspiring Presence",
					"local_notes": "The Ethereal's half of the Devotion pairing. T'au and T'au auxiliaries in sight or hearing of an Ethereal satisfy the Accessibility on Devotion to the Ethereals (Fearlessness +2, Higher Purpose +1) and react to the Ethereal at +4; non-T'au see only the Social Regard.\n\nLore: An Ethereal calms and steels the T'au around them: Fire Warriors under fire stay in place, and envoys see counsel followed. Many in the fiction suspect a pheromonal effect; it is never confirmed. Codex: T'au Empire background.\n\nDesign: Charisma 4 (B41, 5/level = 20) with Accessibility -20% (only T'au and their auxiliaries) = 16, plus Social Regard (Venerated) 3 (B87, 5/level) = 15: 31 points. Social Regard is separate because even foreign envoys treat an Ethereal with care; the Charisma is the aura proper. A true area buff was rejected as unbuildable from Basic Set traits without inventing new modifiers.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Social",
						"Xenos",
						"T'au"
					],
					"container_type": "meta_trait",
					"children": [
						{
							"id": "tnVb3kalnuOUeTbCs",
							"name": "Charisma (T'au and Auxiliaries)",
							"reference": "B41",
							"local_notes": "+4 to reactions and to Diplomacy, Leadership, Public Speaking, Politics and similar Influence rolls with T'au and their auxiliaries.\n\nLore: The Ethereal's pull on T'au society.\n\nDesign: Charisma 4 (B41, 5/level = 20), Accessibility -20% = 16 points.",
							"tags": [
								"Advantage",
								"Social",
								"Xenos",
								"T'au"
							],
							"modifiers": [
								{
									"id": "mO5boajNGH635khAK",
									"name": "Accessibility",
									"reference": "B110",
									"local_notes": "Only affects T'au and T'au auxiliaries (Kroot, Vespid, Gue'vesa), not other species.",
									"cost_adj": "-20%"
								}
							],
							"points_per_level": 5,
							"can_level": true,
							"levels": 4
						},
						{
							"id": "tMY0SRlWT5H5eAchN",
							"name": "Social Regard (Venerated Ethereal)",
							"reference": "B87",
							"local_notes": "Venerated: +3 reactions from those who hold Ethereals in awe.\n\nLore: To most T'au, and to many others, an Ethereal is very nearly sacred.\n\nDesign: Social Regard (Venerated) 3 (B87, 5/level) = 15 points.",
							"tags": [
								"Advantage",
								"Social",
								"Xenos",
								"T'au"
							],
							"points_per_level": 5,
							"can_level": true,
							"levels": 3
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T5G7LN0soBufXt6Qn",
			"name": "The Greater Good",
			"local_notes": "T'au'va. Sense of Duty to the T'au Empire and everyone in it, auxiliaries included, and a professional code: obey the chain of command and the Ethereals, do not leave a comrade or ally behind, offer terms before slaughter. This is duty, not fanaticism in the Imperial sense: a T'au can be argued with, and can change sides of a policy if the Ethereals do.\n\nLore: The Greater Good (T'au'va) is the founding creed of the Empire: every caste, and every race that joins, serves the whole. It is a philosophy and a political programme, and it is offered before it is imposed. Codex: T'au Empire background across editions.\n\nDesign: Sense of Duty (Large Group: the Empire) -10 (B153) + Code of Honor (Professional) -5 (B127) = -15 points. Code of Honor is priced as the lightest B127 tier, not the Soldier's -10: the T'au are not depicted as glorying in combat or in personal honour, only in discharge of duty. Entire Race (-15) was rejected because the Greater Good extends past the T'au.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"T'au"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tXFWyX_7LjJawV_dD",
					"name": "Sense of Duty (T'au Empire)",
					"reference": "B153",
					"local_notes": "Will not knowingly act against the Empire's good; roll self-control to put personal interest first.\n\nLore: The Greater Good is a duty to a whole civilisation, not a single master.\n\nDesign: Sense of Duty (B153), Large Group -10.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"T'au"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mDiLsfa71Ply6CDr2",
							"name": "Large Group (the T'au Empire and its allied races)",
							"reference": "B153",
							"cost_adj": "-10"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tOUHGYLB865roVXK0",
					"name": "Code of Honor (Greater Good)",
					"reference": "B127",
					"local_notes": "Obey caste duty and the Ethereals; do not abandon comrades or allies; offer terms before annihilation. Breaking it is a reaction penalty from other T'au and a self-esteem crisis.\n\nLore: Each caste has its own code; the Fire caste's is a soldier's, close to B127's Professional code but with a duty to offer surrender first. Background.\n\nDesign: Code of Honor (Professional) -5 (B127).",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"T'au"
					],
					"base_points": -5
				}
			]
		}
	],
